      en: "Cotton was once the 'white gold' of Paraguay and remains an important crop, particularly in the Chaco and northern regions. In the Chaco, cotton cultivation is adapted to the semi-arid conditions, providing a vital source of income for both small-scale farmers and large cooperatives. The fiber produced is known for its high quality and is used both in the national textile industry and for international export. While the sector has faced challenges from global price fluctuations and pests, it remains a key component of Paraguay's agricultural heritage. Modern efforts focus on improving yields and sustainability, ensuring that cotton continues to support the livelihoods of thousands of Paraguayan families. Geography K7 — fiber crops and rural economy."
